## 6. Functional Requirements

### FR-01 — Parent Authentication

**Priority:** Must have

Parents must authenticate before accessing the household history.

Acceptance criteria:

- A parent can create or join a private household.
- The household supports exactly two parent accounts in the MVP.
- An invitation mechanism allows the second parent to join.
- Unauthenticated users cannot access baby records.
- Users outside the household cannot access its records.
- A parent cannot access another household's data.

### FR-02 — Voice Capture

**Priority:** Must have

Parents can record a voice message describing one or more baby-related events.

Acceptance criteria:

- The user can start and stop voice capture.
- The application provides clear recording feedback.
- The application converts speech into text.
- The resulting text is available for review.
- The user can cancel the recording.
- The user can correct the transcription before saving.
- The application handles transcription failures without silently creating incorrect records.

Voice capture is the primary user experience, not an optional secondary feature.

### FR-03 — Text Capture

**Priority:** Must have

Parents can manually type a natural-language description.

Acceptance criteria:

- A parent can enter a free-text description.
- The text is processed using the same structuring logic as voice input.
- Text capture does not require the user to complete a predefined form.

### FR-04 — AI Event Extraction

**Priority:** Must have

The AI converts natural-language descriptions into structured event records.

Example input:

"He had 90 ml of formula at 2 AM."

Expected structured output:

- Event type: Bottle feeding
- Amount: 90
- Unit: ml
- Time: 02:00
- Feeding type: Formula

Acceptance criteria:

- The model returns data following a defined schema.
- The model must not invent missing values.
- Unknown values remain unknown.
- Ambiguous information is identified.
- Multiple events in one message can be recognised.
- The parent can review and correct extracted information before saving.
- Invalid or incomplete model responses must not silently become saved records.

### FR-05 — Review and Confirmation

**Priority:** Must have

Parents must be able to review AI-generated records before they become part of the shared history.

Acceptance criteria:

- The application displays the extracted event details.
- The parent can confirm the record.
- The parent can edit individual fields.
- The parent can discard the proposed record.
- Ambiguous information is clearly highlighted.
- The application does not force a parent to provide information that was not included in the original input.

### FR-06 — Event Timeline

**Priority:** Must have

Confirmed events appear in a chronological timeline.

Initial event categories:

| Category | Example information |
|---|---|
| Breastfeeding | Side, duration, time |
| Bottle feeding | Amount, unit, milk type |
| Sleep | Start time, end time, duration |
| Wet nappy | Time, optional notes |
| Dirty nappy | Time, optional notes |
| General note | Free-text observations |

Acceptance criteria:

- Events appear in chronological order.
- Each event displays its available information.
- Missing fields do not prevent an event from appearing.
- Parents can filter the timeline by category.
- Parents can open an event to view its details.
- The timeline does not imply that missing events did not happen.

### FR-07 — Shared Synchronisation

**Priority:** Must have

Both parents must see the same confirmed history on their own devices.

Acceptance criteria:

- A record created by Parent 1 becomes available to Parent 2.
- A record created by Parent 2 becomes available to Parent 1.
- Updates to an existing record are synchronised.
- Deleted records are reflected on both devices.
- The application communicates when changes have not yet synchronised.
- The application handles temporary connectivity loss.
- The application avoids creating duplicate records when a request is retried.
- Conflicting edits must not silently overwrite one another.

### FR-08 — Record Management

**Priority:** Must have

Parents can manage previously saved events.

Acceptance criteria:

- Both parents can edit a record.
- Both parents can delete a record.
- Changes are synchronised across devices.
- The application records creation and update timestamps.
- The application identifies which parent created a record.

### FR-09 — Historical Search

**Priority:** Should have

Parents can retrieve previously recorded information.

Example questions:

- "When was the last bottle feed?"
- "How much formula did he have yesterday?"
- "Show me the breastfeeding sessions from this morning."

Acceptance criteria:

- Search results are based on saved records.
- Results show the relevant event and timestamp.
- The system does not present an unrecorded event as a fact.
- When no matching information exists, the application clearly says so.
- The system distinguishes between "not recorded" and "did not happen."

### FR-10 — Optional Recording Suggestions

**Priority:** Should have

NapNook may suggest recording an event, but must never make parents feel obligated to do so.

Acceptance criteria:

- Suggestions are optional.
- Parents can dismiss suggestions.
- Dismissing a suggestion does not create repeated pressure.
- Missing records are not treated as proof that an activity was missed.
- The product does not use guilt-inducing language.

