## 8. Privacy, Security and Data Management

Baby-related records are sensitive personal information. Privacy must be considered from the beginning of the product design.

### 8.1 Access Control

- Only the two authenticated parents can access household records.
- Household membership must be enforced server-side.
- API requests must verify user permissions.
- API keys must never be exposed in the iOS application.
- Data must not be accessible through predictable or unauthorised identifiers.

### 8.2 Data Minimisation

- Collect only information needed for the product.
- Avoid sending unnecessary personal information to external AI services.
- Do not include real baby data in development or public portfolio demonstrations.
- Use synthetic data in testing and documentation.

### 8.3 AI Processing

The product may use an external AI service to interpret voice transcriptions and text.

Requirements:

- Disclose when information is processed by an external AI provider.
- Minimise information sent to the model.
- Avoid sending raw audio when a transcription is sufficient.
- Keep AI API credentials on a secure backend.
- Define data retention and deletion behaviour before launch.

### 8.4 Storage and Synchronisation

The preferred architectural direction is local-first with secure synchronisation between the two parent devices.

This is a product constraint, not a final technical architecture decision.

The technical design must establish:

- What data is stored locally.
- What data is synchronised.
- How data is encrypted in transit and at rest.
- How authentication and household permissions work.
- How offline changes are reconciled.
- How records are backed up and recovered.
- How parents can delete their data.

### 8.5 User Control

Parents must be able to:

- Edit their records.
- Delete individual records.
- Understand which information is stored.
- Request deletion of household data.
- Understand what information is sent to external AI services.

